# The register-routes C10 landing: the render entries offer an unknown main card to the format

The row's recorded unblock was: "the render entry points offer an unknown
main card to the format, so the arm answers :not_found and writes the 404
the way the pinned one does." This landing does exactly that, on the
candidate branch deploy/full-candidate-20261104 at tip a1b8a215, in the
exclusive worktree for branch port/not-found-404-20261027.

## What the code does now

- `src/card/render/page.ts`, both render entry points
  (renderPageBytesWithPageConfigInner and renderOutsideOfLayout): the
  controller's new-card fetch (fetchByName with the request's new-card
  arguments) admits the phantom on EVERY posture. The admission no longer
  gates on `RenderEngine#renderAbsentCards`, so the format's main+root
  viewForUnknown arm (format.ts, the block that writes pageErrorStatus and
  answers `canCreate ? "new" : "not_found"`) is what answers for an
  unknown MAIN card from both entries. The residual `card not found`
  throws at the two fetch sites fire only when a fetcher cannot build a
  phantom at all (a test stub): nothing then exists to offer the format.
  The admitted phantom's own bare renders (the prepare pass, the modal
  prepare) carry `absentCard: true` this render alone
  (HistoryRenderParams#absentCard), so the offer does not die at the
  prepare pass.
- The two entry-side `:new` pre-substitutions are gone. Ruby renders the
  body with the requested or layout's own main view and the arm
  substitutes at dispatch (view/permission.rb altered_view ->
  format/error.rb view_for_unknown); the voo keeps the REQUESTED view.
  The old explicit "new" bypassed that: :new is itself a perms-:none
  view, so permission.rb skip_check? short-circuited the substitution and
  a not-creatable viewer took the create-form denial (error.rb ok? maps
  :update to :create on a new card) where Ruby's arm answers the
  not_found page at 404. With the arm answering, the may-create slot JSON
  also matches the pinned recording (voo.raw_view keeps the requested
  view). The former `card not found` raise at the outside-of-layout
  create gate and the twin gate inside the prepare pass are removed with
  the same reason: Ruby raises nothing there, the arm is the answer.
- `src/card/render/format.ts`: the viewForUnknown doc comment now states
  the arm is reached from the page entries on every posture and keeps the
  measurement trail (MEASURED SKIP-AND-NAME, the earlier landing's ten
  moved host assertions, the serving boot's flag seat, this landing).
- `vm-staging-server.mjs`: the non-stock boot's page branch reads the
  page's own status (renderPageWithStatus) instead of the unconditional
  200. That 200 was the early-refusal contract (a missing card raised, so
  a returned page was always 200); with the arm answering, the format's
  own answer is the contract on both boot postures. This resolves the
  divergence the staging wildcard suite had pinned as named
  (tests/deploy/staging-bad-address-wildcard-endpoint.test.ts, the known
  junction's 200 where the pinned deck answers 404).

## Why the landing is safe to make now (the measurement trail, corrected)

The row's earlier finding ("the offer moved TEN pinned host assertions, a
serving-surface change with the error page's render budget behind it")
was measured before commit 1640bd872 seated `renderAbsentCards = true` on
every engine the serving boot builds (bunny-edge-deck-boot.ts bootDeck).
The serving paths (the edge's shared deck engine, each live render's own
engines, the wildcard bad_address arm, the Node staging host's stock
profile) therefore already answered unknown main cards through the arm;
what refused the card was the ENGINE-DEFAULT posture (the flag off: the
parity test's storage.engine and the deploy tests' realDeck engines).
This landing puts the offer inside the entries themselves, so the posture
an engine boots with no longer decides the arm's reachability. On
flag-seated engines the behavior is unchanged.

